Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Arrow Seven Does Not Break the Drone
- Story & Context: During the first vector test, Elin selects an airflow sheet marked with seven directional arrows. Marcus interprets the seventh arrow as a rule that the drums must disappear for one beat every seven cycles while the smallpipe drone continues without interruption. Rowan builds a prepared-spinet pattern from two clipped Dorian dyads, and Sora holds breath-heavy contrabass-flute notes above the bass sequence without matching its root directly.

As the track develops, the rhythmic mutes become more obvious, but the smallpipes never acknowledge them. The listener begins to hear the drone as the stable reference and the drum machine as the unstable element. In the final section, Marcus removes the entire beat for two bars while Elin continues the same chanter phrase, proving that the melodic pressure was carrying the piece all along.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cream airflow diagram marked with seven white grease-pencil arrows on a faded teal control desk, Northumbrian smallpipes resting across the paper, compact electro sequencer and prepared-spinet key visible nearby, dark wind-tunnel opening in the distance. Spoken fragments limited to “seven arrows,” “do not break the drone,” “leave the grid out,” and “pressure stays.”

Track 2: Felt Wedge Under Middle C
- Story & Context: Rowan discovers that a small felt wedge under the spinet’s middle-C string shortens the note into a dry wooden snap with almost no tonal tail. Marcus assigns that attack to an offbeat sequencer role normally filled by a synthetic click, but Rowan insists on performing every strike live.

Elin responds with short keyed chanter ornaments that begin immediately after the damped C, while Sora uses contrabass-flute tongue stops as low acoustic punctuation. Halfway through, Rowan removes the felt wedge. The restored spinet resonance suddenly spills across several drum steps and destabilizes the precision of the groove. The final sixteen bars replace the wedge and return the sound to its clipped state, making a tiny physical preparation function like a full arrangement change.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro documentary photograph of a felt wedge touching the middle-C string inside a compact spinet, smallpipes chanter keys in soft focus, contrabass-flute headjoint and metal solenoid plate nearby, cream engineering paper beneath the instrument. Spoken fragments limited to “middle C,” “leave the felt in,” “let it ring once,” and “clip it again.”

Track 3: Solenoid Four Fires Into Silence
- Story & Context: One brass plate in Marcus’s solenoid rack produces a sharper attack than the others and is labeled number four. He programs it to strike only after a complete one-beat drum mute, so the physical metal hit becomes the first event that re-establishes the grid.

Elin holds a long smallpipe phrase across each silence, changing only the grace-note pattern. Rowan plays almost no harmony, using muted single notes as response markers, while Sora sustains a low contrabass-flute tone whose breath noise remains audible even when the electronic bass disappears. Near the end, solenoid four fails to trigger. The ensemble leaves the restart empty and continues anyway, turning a missing mechanical cue into the final structural variation.

The EP ends with the smallpipe drones continuing for several seconds after every sequenced element has stopped.
